[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Inter-VLAN routing between VLAN 20 (172.16.20.0/24) and VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24) is working as designed. From the affected host (Laptop0, 172.16.20.10), ICMP to a VLAN 10 workstation succeeds: ping to PC4 (172.16.10.4) returned 0% loss with ~1.6 ms RTT, and ping to the VLAN 10 gateway (172.16.10.1) also succeeded. On router0, the L3 SVI/sub-interfaces are present and up: eth0.20 has 172.16.20.1/24 and eth0.10 has 172.16.10.1/24 (ip addr shows both UP/LOWER_UP). Router0’s packet filters are permissive: iptables default policies are ACCEPT and nftables contains only a local nat table, no filter rules that would block VLAN 20 -> VLAN 10. Laptop0’s routing is correct (default via 172.16.20.1). Additional tests from another VLAN 10 host to VLAN 10 peers also show normal intra-VLAN connectivity.

Mechanism: With both SVIs up and no ACLs, router0 will forward 172.16.20/24 traffic to 172.16.10/24 directly, which matches the successful end-to-end pings observed.

Localization: No fault found on router0 (eth0.10/eth0.20) nor on Laptop0’s NIC or default gateway path.

Root cause: No network-side inter-VLAN routing fault was present; the reported outage could not be reproduced and may have been a transient host-side or ARP condition.

Corrective fix: No corrective action required. If the symptom recurs, have the user capture arp -n and traceroute during the event, and verify local host firewalls; otherwise continue to monitor. 
=== END ===